```{r}
library(broom)
library(dplyr)

summary_model <- tibble(
  Model = c(
    "Poverty",
    "Income",
    "Rurality",
    "Population Density"
  ),
  R2 = c(
    summary(poverty_rate)$r.squared,
    summary(median_income)$r.squared,
    summary(rucc)$r.squared,
    summary(population_density)$r.squared
  ),
  Adj_R2 = c(
    summary(poverty_rate)$adj.r.squared,
    summary(median_income)$adj.r.squared,
    summary(rucc)$adj.r.squared,
    summary(population_density)$adj.r.squared
  ),
  P_Value = c(
    coef(summary(poverty_rate))[2,4],
    coef(summary(median_income))[2,4],
    coef(summary(rucc))[2,4],
    coef(summary(population_density))[2,4]
  )
)

summary_model
```

# The model shows that each of the variables are stastically significant because the p-values around 0.05 or less, demostrating that they are all small, but the R2 values are all less than 5%, suggesting that not only one variable is able to predict the recovery ratio, and we must use a multiple regression to highlight the prediction more clearly. 

```{r}
#Create a multiple regression
multiple_covid <- lm(
  recovery_ratio_covid ~
    poverty_rate_2019 +
    median_hh_income_2019 +
    total_population_2019 +
    factor(rucc_2013_2019) +
    log_population_density_2019 +
    proprietor_share_2019 +
    net_migration_rate_2019 +
    pct_bachelors_plus_2019 +
    birth_rate_2019,
  data = county_data
)

summary(multiple_covid)
```

# The multiple regression model indicated that median household income, rurality, proprietor share, net migration rate, bachelor's degree attainment, and birth rate were significant predictors of county recovery following COVID-19. In contrast, total population and population density were not statistically significant after controlling for the other socioeconomic variables. Poverty rate showed only marginal significance (p = 0.092), suggesting its effect diminished once the remaining predictors were included.

```{r}
lasso_data <- county_data %>%
  select(
    recovery_ratio_covid,
    poverty_rate_2019,
    median_hh_income_2019,
    total_population_2019,
    rucc_2013_2019,
    log_population_density_2019,
    proprietor_share_2019,
    net_migration_rate_2019,
    pct_bachelors_plus_2019,
    birth_rate_2019
  ) %>%
  na.omit()
```

```{r}
x <- model.matrix(
  recovery_ratio_covid ~
    poverty_rate_2019 +
    median_hh_income_2019 +
    total_population_2019 +
    factor(rucc_2013_2019) +
    log_population_density_2019 +
    proprietor_share_2019 +
    net_migration_rate_2019 +
    pct_bachelors_plus_2019 +
    birth_rate_2019,
  data = county_data
)[,-1]

y <- lasso_data$recovery_ratio_covid
```

```{r}
complete <- complete.cases(x, y)

x <- x[complete, ]
y <- y[complete]

set.seed(42)

lasso_cv <- cv.glmnet(
  x,
  y,
  alpha = 1
)
plot(lasso_cv)
lasso_cv$lambda.1se
coef(lasso_cv, s = "lambda.1se")
```

